Build (src/CMakeLists.txt, target mlkem_native)
-----------------------------------------------
One C file, mlkem768.c. No include directory is added for this tree: upstream's params.h,
poly.h and symmetric.h share names with pqcrypto/ml-dsa-65's headers, whose directory is a
PUBLIC include directory of pqcrypto. The upstream sources only use quote-includes relative to
themselves; xcoin_config.h is found as "crypto/mlkem-native/xcoin_config.h" through src/, and
crypto/mlkem768.cpp includes the public header by full path with the same MLK_CONFIG_FILE.
Warnings: upstream's own set (test/mk/config.mk: -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow
-Wmissing-prototypes -Wredundant-decls -Wpointer-arith -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion) plus
-Wcast-align, -Wundef and -Wvla, and the node's hardening flags where the C compiler takes them
(the same function as pqcrypto). The sources compile without a single warning under that set
on Apple clang 21 (arm64); no warning is suppressed and no per-file flag is needed.
The object exports the seven public functions (xcoin_mlkem768_keypair_derand, _keypair,
_enc_derand, _enc, _dec, _check_pk, _check_sk) and upstream's FIPS-202 layer
(xcoin_mlkem768_shake128_*, _shake256*, _sha3_*, _keccakf1600*), which upstream gives no
linkage qualifier; all carry the xcoin_mlkem768_ prefix, so none can collide with PQClean's
unprefixed shake128/shake256/sha3_256.

Evidence
--------
src/test/mlkem768_tests.cpp, with the vectors in src/test/data/ml_kem_768_fips203.json
(generated by contrib/testgen/gen_mlkem768_vectors.py, which also recomputes every vector with
test/functional/test_framework/crypto/mlkem.py):
  - every ML-KEM-768 case of NIST ACVP ML-KEM-keyGen-FIPS203 (25) and
    ML-KEM-encapDecap-FIPS203 (25 encapsulation, 10 decapsulation including 5 modified
    ciphertexts, 10 encapsulation-key checks, 10 decapsulation-key checks), byte for byte;
  - Go crypto/mlkem TestAccumulated, 100 and 10,000 iterations;
  - C2SP CCTV ML-KEM-768: all 780 bad encapsulation keys (modulus/), the unlucky-sampling
    ek (Encaps and Decaps; its d was found under the FIPS 203 draft's key generation, so
    KeyGen(d, z) is not a FIPS 203 vector) and the strcmp vector;
  - the OpenSSL 3.6.4 vectors pinned in the test framework's mlkem.py, 50 cases computed by
    mlkem.py itself, and XIP-4's own ML-KEM known answers and V6 bad keys.

Re-vendoring
------------
Take the new release's tarball, check its SHA-256 against the release, copy the files listed
above (and any new file mlkem/mlkem_native.c includes for a portable-C build) to the same
paths, diff upstream's mlkem/mlkem_native_config.h against the previous release's for new
options that xcoin_config.h must decide on, record the commit, tarball hash and file hashes
here, rebuild (no new warnings allowed), and run `test_bitcoin -t mlkem768_tests` and
`bench_bitcoin -filter='MLKEM768.*'`.
